  const summaryHTML = () => `
    <h2>How the three topics fit together</h2>
    <p>All three topics are about <strong>mass transport</strong>: the bulk flow of a liquid that carries substances to where they are needed (Unit 1, 3.1.5.1). In a mammal the heart pumps blood; part of it flows through the <strong>coronary arteries</strong> to the heart muscle itself (3.2.6.2; Unit 1, 3.1.5.3). <strong>3.2.7</strong> is what happens when that supply fails: atheroma, thrombosis and myocardial infarction. In a plant there is no pump: water moves up the <strong>xylem</strong> down a water potential gradient created by transpiration, and sucrose moves in the <strong>phloem</strong> by mass flow down a hydrostatic pressure gradient created by active loading and osmosis (<strong>3.2.8</strong>; Unit 1, 3.1.4.2 and 3.1.4.3). <strong>3.2.9</strong> shows an animal exploiting that pressure: aphids insert their stylets into sieve tubes, and in doing so they carry viruses from one immobile plant to another.</p>
    <h3>The chain in six links</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Oxygen supply</strong> <span class="badge">Unit 1</span>: oxygen diffuses from alveolar air into the blood, loads onto haemoglobin, and is carried by mass transport to respiring cells, where it is unloaded and used to make ATP. <span class="muted">Unit 1 3.1.5.2, 3.1.5.3, 3.1.4.3</span></li>
      <li><strong>Supply to the heart muscle</strong>: the wall of the heart is too thick to be supplied by diffusion from the blood in its chambers, so the coronary arteries, branches of the aorta, carry blood to it. <span class="muted">3.2.6.2; Unit 1 3.1.5.3</span></li>
      <li><strong>When the supply fails</strong> <span class="badge teal">required</span>: atheroma (lipid-containing material within artery walls) increases the risk of thrombosis and aneurysm; a thrombus blocking a coronary artery interrupts blood flow to cardiac muscle, causing a myocardial infarction. Diet, blood cholesterol, smoking and high blood pressure are risk factors. <span class="muted">3.2.7.1, 3.2.7.2, 3.2.1.2</span></li>
      <li><strong>Water in the xylem</strong> <span class="badge teal">required</span>: water enters root hairs by osmosis, crosses the cortex by the apoplastic and symplastic pathways, passes the endodermis and enters the xylem; root pressure gives a small push, and transpiration pulls water up. <span class="muted">3.2.8.1; Unit 1 3.1.4.2, 3.1.4.3</span></li>
      <li><strong>Sucrose in the phloem</strong> <span class="badge teal">required</span>: active loading at a source lowers the water potential of the sieve tube sap, water enters by osmosis from the xylem, and the high hydrostatic pressure drives mass flow to sinks. <span class="muted">3.2.8.2, 3.2.2.3; Unit 1 3.1.4.3</span></li>
      <li><strong>Aphids and viruses</strong> <span class="badge teal">required</span>: aphids insert stylets into sieve tubes and the pressurised sap is forced into their gut; because plants are immobile, viruses are usually transmitted by vectors such as aphids, and winged females carry them to new plants. <span class="muted">3.2.9.1, 3.2.9.2, 3.2.1.1</span></li>
    </ol>
    <h3>Three contrasts that examiners reward</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pressure.</strong> Blood in arteries and sap in sieve tubes are under high (positive) pressure; water in the xylem of a transpiring plant is usually under tension. A cut artery or sieve tube leaks; a cut xylem vessel draws in air.</li>
      <li><strong>Energy.</strong> The heart uses ATP to contract; phloem loading and root pressure use ATP for active transport; transpiration and the flow along a sieve tube are passive. Say exactly where ATP is used.</li>
      <li><strong>Gradient.</strong> Name the gradient: water potential (osmosis), hydrostatic pressure (mass flow), concentration (diffusion of oxygen or water vapour).</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Supporting ideas used on this page <span class="badge">supporting</span></h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The cohesion–tension explanation of how transpiration pulls water up; the Casparian strip; sieve tube elements and companion cells.</li>
      <li>Sucrose loading by co-transport with hydrogen ions, which works like the sodium co-transport of glucose in the ileum (3.2.2.3).</li>
      <li>Many plant viruses, once inside a plant, travel in the phloem with the flow of sugars from sources to sinks (Hipper and others, 2013).</li>
      <li>Persistent and non-persistent transmission by aphids.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small muted">This page explains biology. It does not tell anyone their own risk of heart disease and is not medical advice.</p>`;

